Xiri console graphics
======================

These are the canonical sources for the SGI-style console decoration:

    sgiconsole.xcf   Editable GIMP artwork supplied with the console image.
    sgiconsole.png   Exported RGB image used by both conversion tools.
    sgiconsole.wsd   Generated indexed image loaded by wsdecorctl.
    initial.conf    Black-to-Octane-green gradient for the initial appearance.
    framed.conf     Framed image and inset text rectangle for normal startup.
    mkwsd.py        Convert an image to the runtime WSDIMG1 format.
    mkframe.py      Generate the optional compiled-in Newport image header.

sgiconsole.png is the canonical export for both console variants.  Preserve
the editable XCF when exporting an updated PNG.

The supplied image is 1282x1024; its final two columns are black.  The framed
console uses it at 1282x1024 or clips those columns at 1280x1024.  Its text
rectangle is (288,176) through (991,847), inclusive: 704x672 pixels, or 88
columns by 42 rows with the normal 8x16 font.  It lies entirely inside the
uniform grey panel, leaving padding between text and the bevel.

Runtime image
-------------

From the repository root, regenerate or check the runtime image with:

    python3 xiri/graphics/mkwsd.py \
        xiri/graphics/sgiconsole.png xiri/graphics/sgiconsole.wsd
    python3 xiri/graphics/mkwsd.py \
        xiri/graphics/sgiconsole.png xiri/graphics/sgiconsole.wsd --check

The normal share/wscons/themes build installs initial.conf, framed.conf,
sgiconsole.wsd and mkwsd.py from this directory to /usr/share/wscons/themes.
The XCF and PNG are editing sources and are not installed by that target.
The default startup selection remains /usr/share/wscons/themes/framed.conf.
Other display sizes retain the initial gradient if that image cannot load.
See wsdecorctl(8) and tests/dev/wscons/README.runtime-themes for configuration
and testing.

Compiled-in image
-----------------

NEWPORT_FRAME optionally embeds the frame from early console attachment.
Its generated header stays with the driver at
sys/arch/sgimips/gio/newport_frame.h so kernel builds need no artwork tools.
Regenerate or check it from the repository root with:

    python3 xiri/graphics/mkframe.py
    python3 xiri/graphics/mkframe.py --check

The script locates its source and output relative to its own path and also
works when invoked from another directory.  The header records the PNG's
SHA256 and encodes disjoint filled rectangles.  The converter verifies that
they reconstruct every source pixel exactly.  At other display sizes the
compiled-in frame falls back to the gradient and full-screen text area.

Editing requirements
--------------------

Pillow is needed for regeneration only.  Normal builds use the checked-in WSD
image and C header.  All 161 colours in the supplied PNG are preserved.
Newport reserves the RGB332 terminal colours and their cursor complements,
plus the uniform panel's complement, leaving room for at most 223 image colours.

Export an opaque RGB PNG from sgiconsole.xcf.  Keep the text panel uniform and
check its rectangle in both framed.conf and mkframe.py when changing the layout.
The fixed compiled-in layout currently requires a 1282x1024 export.  Regenerate
both outputs and run the console checks described in
tests/dev/wscons/README.newport-frame and tests/dev/wscons/README.runtime-themes.
